6a576814-0f9f-45e9-b0a4-07f0883633f0	\N	E2E-24-S17	Canal transit planning — Suez/Panama (if applicable)	Plan canal transit logistics for services that route through the Suez Canal or Panama Canal. Suez Canal (most common for Middle East services): calculate transit fees based on Suez Canal Net Tonnage (SCNT) — Suez Canal Authority (SCA) publishes annual tariff tables, container vessels pay based on laden TEU count + SCNT; typical cost: $200,000-$700,000 per transit depending on vessel size. Plan convoy joining times — northbound convoy departs from Suez at 03:00-04:00, southbound from Port Said at 06:00-07:00; vessels must arrive at convoy anchorage 4-6 hours before departure. Transit takes 12-16 hours through the 193km canal. Since 2015 expansion, the New Suez Canal allows two-way traffic in the central section, reducing waiting time. Plan for pilot boarding, tugboat assistance (mandatory for large container vessels), and canal documentation (transit papers, crew list, cargo declaration). Panama Canal (for services connecting Pacific/Atlantic): two options — Panamax locks (max 5,000 TEU, beam ≤32.3m) or Neopanamax locks (max 14,000 TEU, beam ≤51.25m). Booking system: auction slots (premium for guaranteed transit date) vs regular slots (may wait 1-7 days). Transit fee: $100,000-$800,000 depending on vessel size and lock type. Plan fresh water fee (Gatun Lake draft restrictions during dry season).	\N	ai_agent	\N	\N	\N	"{\\"mode\\":\\"ai_with_tools\\",\\"systemPromptExtra\\":\\"You are a Canal Planning Agent. Plan Suez/Panama Canal transits — booking, tolls, restrictions. Provide JSON with: canalTransits, tolls, restrictions, alternativeRoutes.\\"}"	\N	Canal Planning Agent	8.00	t	\N	\N	system	\N	1	t	"[{\\"field\\":\\"Port Rotation and Schedule (from step 16)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Schedule Draft (Step 16)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Vessel SCNT/Panama Tonnage Measurements\\",\\"source\\":\\"Vessel Particulars / Tonnage Certificate\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Expected Laden TEU Count per Transit\\",\\"source\\":\\"Demand Forecast (Step 1)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Canal Authority Tariff Tables (current year)\\",\\"source\\":\\"SCA / ACP Published Tariffs\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Convoy Schedules and Anchorage Requirements\\",\\"source\\":\\"Canal Authority / Agents\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Canal Booking Availability (for Panama)\\",\\"source\\":\\"ACP Booking System\\",\\"required\\":false}]"	"[\\"Canal Transit Plan per Voyage (convoy time, transit direction, pilot boarding point)\\",\\"Transit Fee Calculation per Voyage (SCNT-based + TEU surcharge)\\",\\"Annual Canal Cost Estimate (52 transits × fee per transit)\\",\\"Anchorage Arrival Window (hours before convoy departure)\\",\\"Canal Documentation Checklist\\",\\"Panama Lock Selection (Panamax vs Neopanamax) and Booking Strategy\\"]"	"[\\"Transit fees calculated using current-year tariff (not outdated rates)\\",\\"Convoy joining times achievable given vessel arrival from previous port\\",\\"Annual canal cost included in service P&L (material cost item — can be 5-15% of voyage cost)\\",\\"Canal draft restrictions respected (Suez: 20.1m tropical fresh water; Panama: varies by season)\\",\\"Panama lock selection matches vessel beam (Panamax max 32.3m, Neopanamax max 51.25m)\\"]"	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-04-01 12:32:40.557014+00	2026-04-01 12:32:40.557014+00
4c30af33-319b-48d4-ae3b-ccc118c32567	\N	E2E-24-S18	Speed & fuel analysis per leg	Perform detailed speed and fuel analysis for every leg on the rotation. For each leg (port A → port B): calculate optimal speed considering the schedule window (time available = ETD port A to ETA port B minus pilot boarding time), distance, weather conditions (seasonal: SW monsoon Jun-Sep in Indian Ocean adds 5-10% to consumption, NE monsoon Nov-Feb adds 3-5%), and current (favorable or adverse — Red Sea/Suez approach has strong northerly currents). Determine fuel type per leg: within ECA (Emission Control Areas) — if any ports are in ECA zones (currently North Sea, Baltic, North America, US Caribbean), vessel must burn LSMGO (0.10% sulfur) or have scrubber; outside ECA: VLSFO (0.50% sulfur) or HFO (3.50% sulfur with scrubber). Calculate bunker consumption per leg using vessel-specific SFOC (Specific Fuel Oil Consumption) curves — consumption increases exponentially with speed (roughly: doubling speed = 8× fuel consumption). At eco-speed (14-16 knots for typical container vessel), consumption is typically 80-120 MT/day for 4,000-8,000 TEU vessels. Calculate total bunker cost per round voyage using bunker prices at planned bunkering ports (prices vary significantly — Singapore typically cheapest, Middle East competitive, Mediterranean premium). Optimize speed per leg to minimize fuel cost while maintaining schedule — if a leg has slack time, slow down to save bunker (slow steaming).	\N	ai_agent	\N	\N	\N	"{\\"mode\\":\\"ai_with_tools\\",\\"systemPromptExtra\\":\\"You are a Speed/Fuel Agent. Analyze speed and fuel consumption per leg of the schedule. Provide JSON with: legAnalysis (array with leg, speed, consumption, emissionsPerTeu).\\"}"	\N	Speed/Fuel Optimization Agent	8.00	t	\N	\N	system	\N	1	t	"[{\\"field\\":\\"52-Week Proforma Schedule with ETA/ETD (from step 16)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Schedule Draft (Step 16)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Port-to-Port Distances per Leg (nautical miles)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Admiralty / Routing Database\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Vessel SFOC Curves per Assigned Vessel (consumption vs speed)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Vessel Particulars / Sea Trials\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Seasonal Weather Factors per Leg (monsoon, wind, current)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Meteorological Data / Historical Noon Reports\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Bunker Prices at Planned Bunkering Ports (IFO/VLSFO/LSMGO)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Ship & Bunker / Procurement\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"ECA Zone Map (where LSMGO is required)\\",\\"source\\":\\"IMO MARPOL Annex VI\\",\\"required\\":true}]"	"[\\"Optimal Speed per Leg (knots)\\",\\"Fuel Type per Leg (HFO/VLSFO/LSMGO based on ECA requirements)\\",\\"Daily Fuel Consumption per Leg (MT/day by fuel type)\\",\\"Total Fuel Consumption per Round Voyage (MT by fuel type)\\",\\"Bunker Cost per Round Voyage ($)\\",\\"Annual Fuel Budget for Service (52 voyages × cost per voyage)\\",\\"CII Impact Assessment (actual emission intensity vs required rating)\\",\\"Slow Steaming Opportunities (legs with schedule slack)\\"]"	"[\\"Required speed on every leg is at or below vessel eco-speed\\",\\"Fuel type switches to LSMGO within ECA zones (if applicable)\\",\\"SFOC curves used are vessel-specific (not generic fleet average)\\",\\"Weather factors applied using seasonal data (not annual average)\\",\\"CII rating meets minimum C threshold at calculated operating profile\\",\\"Bunkering plan ensures vessel never drops below safety reserve (typically 3-5 days at eco-speed)\\"]"	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-04-01 12:32:40.560966+00	2026-04-01 12:32:40.560966+00
ef987f8a-8b3c-4d55-aff7-32034923dd07	\N	E2E-24-S20	Schedule publication approval: approve schedule for market release	Final approval gate before the schedule is published to the market. This is the most consequential gate in the entire flow — once published, the schedule becomes a binding commitment to customers, partners, terminals, and regulators. Decision-makers review: the complete 52-week proforma, schedule quality validation results (transit time competitiveness, feasibility, disruption resilience), fleet financial assessment (cost per voyage, break-even load factor), speed/fuel analysis (CII compliance, bunker budget), canal transit plan (if applicable), and berth window confirmations. Approval criteria: (1) Schedule Quality Score ≥ 75; (2) Transit times competitive on key port pairs; (3) No unresolved feasibility issues; (4) Bunker budget aligned with P&L projection; (5) All terminal agreements signed; (6) First sailing date achievable with current vessel positioning. If approved: schedule status changes to 'approved for publication', commercial teams begin rate filing, EDI schedules are prepared for transmission to customers and portals (INTTRA, CargoSmart), and marketing materials are prepared. The publication itself happens in step 21 (system automated). If rejected: specific issues must be resolved (e.g., adjust rotation, renegotiate terminal windows, change vessel assignment) and schedule resubmitted.	\N	human	gate	\N	\N	"{\\"mode\\":\\"gate\\"}"	approval	VP Operations / CCO	24.00	t	\N	\N	system	\N	1	t	"[{\\"field\\":\\"Complete 52-Week Proforma Schedule\\",\\"source\\":\\"Schedule Draft (Step 16)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Schedule Quality Validation Report (from step 19)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Schedule Validation (Step 19)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Speed & Fuel Analysis Summary (from step 18)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Speed/Fuel Analysis (Step 18)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Canal Transit Plan (from step 17, if applicable)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Canal Planning (Step 17)\\",\\"required\\":false},{\\"field\\":\\"Fleet Financial Assessment (from step 13)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Fleet Financials (Step 13)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Terminal Agreement Confirmations (from step 15)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Port Windows (Step 15)\\",\\"required\\":true}]"	"[\\"Schedule Publication Approval (approved/rejected with rationale)\\",\\"Approved Schedule Version Number\\",\\"Publication Authorization (triggers step 21 system publication)\\",\\"First Sailing Date Confirmation\\",\\"Rate Filing Authorization (for commercial team)\\",\\"Conditions or Required Modifications (if rejected)\\"]"	"[\\"Schedule Quality Score ≥ 75 before approval\\",\\"All terminal agreements confirmed and signed\\",\\"No unresolved critical issues from validation report\\",\\"Approval documented with named approvers and effective date\\",\\"Schedule version number assigned for change tracking\\"]"	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-04-01 12:32:40.568604+00	2026-04-01 12:32:40.568604+00
1012da6b-f8f8-47b1-b3ff-97e4203a5371	\N	E2E-24-S22	Vessel schedule activation (create operational records)	Create operational vessel schedule records for each vessel assigned to the service. While the proforma schedule (step 16) is a planning document, vessel schedule records are the operational truth — they track actual vessel positions, ETAs, and operational status throughout the voyage lifecycle. For each vessel on the service, create: vessel schedule header (vessel → service assignment, effective date range), voyage records (one per round voyage, linked to voyage number from proforma), and per-port-call records with planned ETA/ETD (copied from proforma, updated to actual as voyage progresses). Each vessel schedule record carries: vessel IMO number, voyage number, port call sequence, planned ETA/ETD, berthing terminal, expected cargo operations (load/discharge TEU count), and operational status (scheduled → approaching → arrived → alongside → departed). These records are the source of truth for: customer tracking (ETA updates on customer portal), operations management (port call planning), and performance monitoring (actual vs planned comparison). Vessel schedules are updated in real-time as AIS position data flows in and operations teams report actual arrival/departure times.	\N	system	\N	\N	\N	"{\\"mode\\":\\"ai_with_tools\\",\\"systemPromptExtra\\":\\"You are an Activation Agent. Create operational records for the new schedule. Provide JSON with: voyagesCreated, systemUpdated.\\"}"	\N	System (Automated)	0.10	t	\N	\N	system	\N	1	t	"[{\\"field\\":\\"52-Week Proforma Schedule (from step 16, now published)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Schedule Publication (Step 21)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Vessel-to-Voyage Mapping (from step 16)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Schedule Draft (Step 16)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Named Vessel Details (IMO, name, flag, class)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Fleet Deployment (Steps 11-12)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Terminal Assignments per Port Call (from step 15)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Port Windows (Step 15)\\",\\"required\\":true}]"	"[\\"Vessel Schedule Records (one per vessel × voyage combination)\\",\\"Port Call Records (one per port call with planned ETA/ETD)\\",\\"Voyage Status Initialization (all voyages set to 'scheduled')\\",\\"AIS Tracking Integration Activated per Vessel\\",\\"Customer Tracking Portal Updated with Vessel Details\\"]"	"[\\"Vessel schedule records created for all vessels × all voyages\\",\\"Port call count per voyage matches rotation port count\\",\\"Planned ETA/ETD matches proforma schedule exactly\\",\\"AIS tracking returning position data for each vessel\\",\\"Customer portal shows vessel name and tracking for each sailing\\"]"	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-04-01 12:32:40.572953+00	2026-04-01 12:32:40.572953+00
379ca09e-2c82-468f-911c-29c752d4a60c	\N	E2E-24-S23	Port rotation setup (ETA/ETD per port call)	Create detailed port rotation records that serve as the operational blueprint for each port call. While vessel schedule records (step 22) focus on the vessel's journey, port rotation records focus on port-side operations. For each port call on each voyage: create port rotation record with: arrival berth (from terminal agreement), pilot boarding time (typically 1-2 hours before berth), estimated cargo operations (planned load count, planned discharge count in TEU — derived from booking forecast and empty repositioning plan), planned crane deployment (number of cranes from terminal agreement), estimated port stay duration (moves ÷ crane productivity), cargo cut-off time (typically 24-48 hours before ETD for physical cut-off, 72 hours for documentation cut-off, 24 hours for VGM cut-off — varies by port and terminal), and departure draft calculation (estimated based on loaded container weight). Port rotation records are essential for: terminal pre-notification (terminals need 72-hour advance stowage plan), port agent briefing (agents arrange pilotage, towage, port health, immigration), and cut-off management (system blocks late bookings after cut-off time).	\N	system	\N	\N	\N	"{\\"mode\\":\\"ai_with_tools\\",\\"systemPromptExtra\\":\\"You are a Port Rotation Agent. Set up ETA/ETD per port call in the operational system. Provide JSON with: portCalls, etaEtdSet.\\"}"	\N	System (Automated)	0.50	t	\N	\N	system	\N	1	t	"[{\\"field\\":\\"Vessel Schedule Records (from step 22)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Vessel Schedule Activation (Step 22)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Terminal Agreements (berth, cranes, productivity per port)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Port Windows (Step 15)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Standard Cut-Off Rules per Port (physical/documentation/VGM)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Operations / Port Authority Requirements\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Expected Move Counts per Port Call (from demand forecast)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Demand Analysis (Step 1)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Pilot Boarding Times per Port\\",\\"source\\":\\"Port Authority\\",\\"required\\":true}]"	"[\\"Port Rotation Records (one per port call per voyage)\\",\\"Cargo Cut-Off Times per Port Call (physical, documentation, VGM)\\",\\"Planned Load/Discharge Counts per Port Call (TEU)\\",\\"Crane Deployment Plan per Port Call\\",\\"Port Stay Duration Estimates\\",\\"Pilot Boarding Schedules\\"]"	"[\\"Port rotation records exist for every port call across all voyages\\",\\"Cut-off times are before ETD (physical: ≥24h, documentation: ≥72h, VGM: ≥24h)\\",\\"Planned move counts realistic (not exceeding vessel capacity at any port)\\",\\"Crane allocation matches terminal agreement\\",\\"Pilot boarding time set per port authority requirements\\"]"	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-04-01 12:32:40.576886+00	2026-04-01 12:32:40.576886+00
c1db1a6a-5adb-4c7c-9668-1e24a454d90f	\N	E2E-24-S24	Capacity allocation (contract vs spot vs alliance)	Allocate vessel capacity across commercial channels for each sailing. Total vessel capacity (e.g., 4,500 TEU nominal, ~4,000 TEU effective after weight/stability constraints) must be allocated across: (1) Contract/NAC (Named Account Contract) — capacity reserved for major BCO (Beneficial Cargo Owner) customers under annual contracts with committed volumes and agreed rates. Typically 40-60% of capacity. Allocate based on customer contract MQC (Minimum Quantity Commitment) prorated per sailing; (2) Spot market — capacity available for ad-hoc bookings at prevailing market rates. Typically 20-35% of capacity. Pricing is dynamic based on utilization (higher rates when vessel is filling up); (3) Alliance/VSA slots — capacity allocated to alliance partners per VSA agreement (from step 8). Typically 10-30% if service is a VSA; (4) Reefer — dedicated reefer plug allocation (reefer plugs are a constrained resource — vessel may have 4,500 TEU but only 500 reefer plugs). Allocate reefer across contract/spot proportionally; (5) Special cargo — reserve slots for OOG (out-of-gauge), hazardous, and heavy-lift cargo requiring specific stowage positions. The AI agent optimizes allocation to maximize revenue: contract cargo provides base load certainty, spot cargo captures upside in strong markets, and alliance slots fulfill partnership obligations. Allocation is per sailing, per direction (outbound/inbound), per port pair segment.	\N	ai_agent	\N	\N	\N	"{\\"mode\\":\\"ai_with_tools\\",\\"systemPromptExtra\\":\\"You are a Capacity Allocation Agent. Allocate capacity — contract vs spot vs alliance. Provide JSON with: contractAllocation, spotAllocation, allianceSlots, totalCapacity.\\"}"	\N	Capacity Planning Agent	8.00	t	\N	\N	system	\N	1	t	"[{\\"field\\":\\"Vessel Effective Capacity (TEU and reefer plugs per sailing)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Vessel Particulars / Stowage Plan\\",\\"required\\"