b2aa463b-6547-4f11-b5f7-325ad7cf159a	\N	E2E-24-S25	Rate activation (load rates into tariff engine)	Load freight rates into the tariff engine for the new service. This is human-driven because rate setting is a strategic commercial decision with direct revenue and competitive impact. Rate structure in container shipping: base freight rate (per TEU or per container, by type — 20GP, 40GP, 40HC typically priced at 1:1.5:1.5 ratio, reefer at 2-3× dry rate), plus surcharges (BAF/Bunker Adjustment Factor — fluctuates with fuel prices, CAF/Currency Adjustment Factor, THC/Terminal Handling Charge at origin and destination, documentation fee, B/L fee, seal charge, VGM fee, ERS/Equipment Repositioning Surcharge for imbalanced trades, PSS/Peak Season Surcharge when market is tight, low-sulfur surcharge for IMO 2020 compliance). Rates are set per port pair, per container type, per customer segment: contract rates (lowest — volume commitment), NVOCC rates (mid — forwarder volume), and FAK/Freight-All-Kinds spot rates (highest — no commitment). The pricing team benchmarks against competitor published rates and market indices (Freightos FBX, Drewry WCI, Shanghai SCFI). Rate effective dates align with schedule publication. Rates must be filed with FMC (Federal Maritime Commission) for US trades — 30-day advance notice required. For EU trades, rates are set independently per carrier (no rate-fixing per EU competition law). Configure dynamic pricing rules for spot market: price increases as utilization approaches 85%, premiums for late bookings within 7 days of sailing.	\N	human	\N	\N	\N	"{\\"mode\\":\\"ai_with_tools\\",\\"systemPromptExtra\\":\\"You are a Rate Activation Agent. Load approved rates into the tariff engine. Provide JSON with: ratesActivated, tradeLanes, effectiveDate.\\"}"	\N	Pricing Manager / Head of Commercial	24.00	t	\N	\N	system	\N	1	t	"[{\\"field\\":\\"Service Schedule (port rotation, frequency, first sailing)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Schedule Publication (Step 21)\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"P&L Break-Even Rate per TEU (from step 3/13)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Trade Lane P&L / Fleet Financials\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Competitor Rate Intelligence per Port Pair\\",\\"source\\":\\"Market Intelligence / Freight Indices\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Customer Contract Rates (for NAC customers)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Contract Management / CRM\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"Surcharge Schedule (BAF, CAF, THC, doc fee, etc.)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Pricing Department\\",\\"required\\":true},{\\"field\\":\\"FMC Filing Requirements (if US trade)\\",\\"source\\":\\"Legal / Regulatory\\",\\"required\\":false},{\\"field\\":\\"Dynamic Pricing Rules Configuration\\",\\"source\\":\\"Pricing Department\\",\\"required\\":false}]"	"[\\"Rate Tariff per Port Pair per Container Type (base freight + surcharges)\\",\\"Contract Rate Schedule per NAC Customer\\",\\"NVOCC Rate Schedule\\",\\"FAK/Spot Rate Schedule\\",\\"Surcharge Table (BAF, CAF, THC, documentation, etc.)\\",\\"FMC Filing Confirmation (if US trade)\\",\\"Dynamic Pricing Rules Active\\",\\"Rate Effective Date and Validity Period\\"]"	"[\\"Rates cover all port pairs on the rotation (no gaps)\\",\\"Base freight rate exceeds break-even rate per TEU from P&L analysis\\",\\"Contract rates match signed customer contract terms\\",\\"Spot rates are competitive (within ±10% of market index for trade lane)\\",\\"FMC filing submitted ≥30 days before first sailing (if US trade)\\",\\"Tariff engine returns correct rate for test booking scenarios\\",\\"Dynamic pricing rules fire correctly at configured utilization thresholds\\"]"	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-04-01 12:32:40.585026+00	2026-04-01 12:32:40.585026+00
9ecbac1c-a1cb-4d76-ab1f-22d47853f5e4	\N	E2E-24-S26	Cut-off rules configuration per port	Configure cargo cut-off rules for each port on the rotation. Cut-offs define the deadlines by which cargo, documentation, and VGM (Verified Gross Mass) must be submitted — after cut-off, no further bookings or cargo acceptance for that sailing. Three types of cut-off: (1) Physical/Cargo Cut-Off — deadline for container to arrive at terminal gate. Typically 24-48 hours before ETD. Varies by port: Jebel Ali often 24h, Nhava Sheva 48h, European ports 24h. Terminal-specific — some terminals have different cut-offs for different berths; (2) Documentation Cut-Off — deadline for shipping instructions (SI), customs declarations, and cargo manifest submission. Typically 48-72 hours before ETD. Earlier in ports with strict customs requirements (e.g., US 24-hour advance manifest rule per CBP, EU ICS2 pre-loading advance cargo information); (3) VGM Cut-Off — deadline for Verified Gross Mass submission per SOLAS regulation (mandatory since July 2016). Typically 24 hours before ETD. Two methods: Method 1 (weigh packed container) or Method 2 (calculate from cargo + packaging + container tare weight). Configure cut-off rules per port with: standard offset from ETD (e.g., physical = ETD minus 24h), exceptions for specific cargo types (reefer may have later cut-off due to last-minute loading), holiday adjustments (if cut-off falls on port holiday, move to previous working day), and system enforcement (booking system blocks acceptance after cut-off, EDI rejection for late SI/VGM).	\N	human	\N	\N	\N	"{\\"mode\\":\\"ai_with_tools\\",\\"systemPromptExtra\\":\\"You are a Cut-Off Rules Agent. Configure cut-off rules per port in the schedule. Provide JSON with: cutOffRules (array with port, docCutoff, cargoCutoff, reeferCutoff).\\"}"	\N	Operations Manager / Port Operations Team	8.00	t	\N	\N	system	\N	1	t	"[{\\"field\\":\\"Port Rotation with ETD per Port Call\\",\\"source\\":\\"Port Rotations (Step 23)\\"